\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Effect of BM25 Parameter Tuning}

The results show that tuning BM25 parameters can produce measurable but relatively small improvements.

In the main Pyserini/Lucene experiment, tuning increased NDCG@10 from 0.9142 to 0.9198 and MAP from 0.8799 to 0.8875.

In the local Lucene experiment, tuning increased NDCG@10 from 0.7945 to 0.7962, MRR@10 from 0.8712 to 0.8749, MAP from 0.7421 to 0.7462 and Recall@100 from 0.9479 to 0.9482. However, NDCG@5 decreased from 0.7942 to 0.7935.

The optimized Python implementation obtained higher values than the initial Python implementation across the reported metrics.

\subsection{Efficiency of the Python Implementations}

The initial Python implementation was executed on the large BioASQ14-b 2026 training dataset, containing 5\,729 questions and approximately 48\,949 indexed documents. The complete execution took approximately 30 minutes.

The main computational cost resulted from calculating BM25 scores for every question-document pair, tokenizing thousands of texts and constructing large Python dictionaries for evaluation.

The optimized implementation reduced these costs by precomputing document-side BM25 weights, using sparse matrix multiplication, extracting the top-ranked documents efficiently and evaluating runs in batch.

A smaller dataset was used during development to verify the optimization strategy more quickly. The reported table, however, identifies the Python results as BioASQ14-b 2026 large training results, as required by the experimental record.

\subsection{Interpretation of the Retrieval Scores}

The Pyserini/Lucene system obtains the highest scores in the comparison. However, these results should not be interpreted as a definitive proof that this implementation is intrinsically superior.

The systems may differ in document collection, question subset, analyzer, tokenization, stemming, indexing framework and evaluation procedure. Consequently, the comparison is primarily useful for documenting the development history and identifying the strengths and limitations of each implementation.

\section{Limitations}

Several limitations should be considered.

First, the Pyserini/Lucene configuration used a held-out evaluation subset, while the local Lucene and Python configurations are reported against the BioASQ14-b 2026 large training dataset.

Second, the local Lucene implementation may differ from the main Pyserini/Lucene implementation in processed files, corpus construction, indexing configuration and evaluation setup.

Third, the Python implementation uses a snippet-based corpus rather than complete PubMed articles. This changes the document length distribution, vocabulary and retrieval difficulty.

Fourth, the Lucene collection is reduced and its distractors are not necessarily a random sample of PubMed. Gold documents may be explicitly added to the collection, increasing document coverage.

Fifth, BioASQ relevance judgments are incomplete. Unjudged documents are treated as non-relevant by the evaluation procedure, although some may be relevant in practice.

Finally, execution time depends strongly on the number of questions, indexed documents and implementation details. The original Python execution on the large training dataset took approximately 30 minutes. A smaller dataset was used during development to validate optimization changes.

