\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

This report described the development of four BioASQ document retrieval configurations.

The first configuration used Pyserini/Lucene over a reduced PubMed collection and established the main group baseline. The second configuration adapted a Lucene-based Google Colab pipeline to run locally, replacing cloud-based file access with local paths and enabling greater control over the maximum number of processed files.

The third configuration implemented a transparent Python BM25 pipeline based on BioASQ snippets. The fourth configuration optimized this Python pipeline through sparse matrix scoring, efficient top-$k$ retrieval and batched evaluation.

The local Lucene tuned configuration achieved NDCG@10\,=\,0.7962, MRR@10\,=\,0.8749, MAP\,=\,0.7462 and Recall@100\,=\,0.9482 on the BioASQ14-b 2026 large training dataset.

The Python BM25 results reported in the comparison table were also associated with the BioASQ14-b 2026 large training dataset. The original Python execution took approximately 30 minutes, motivating the subsequent optimization work.

The optimized implementation improved the reported Python retrieval metrics, while the sparse scoring and batched evaluation strategy reduced the computational overhead during development. Smaller data were used during development to verify the optimization before applying it to the large training workflow.

Future work should evaluate all configurations with exactly the same document collection, question split and preprocessing pipeline. Further experiments should include statistical significance testing, per-question-type analysis, stemming and stop-word ablations, larger document collections, dense retrieval models such as MedCPT \cite{jin2023}, hybrid sparse-dense fusion and neural reranking.

